ner, hot temper (crew cooperation, communication), know-it-all (adaptability). So concerns in crew cooperation and communication.\n\nAll have concerns. Which candidate has the best balance? Candidate C has strong decision quality, calmness, technical, attention, and puts concern for others above everything (positive for crew cooperation). Communication difficulty and egocentric are concerns, but maybe less severe than nasty remarks or hot temper. Also not willing to further education is a concern for adaptability. Candidate B's nasty remarks could be severe. Candidate D's hot temper also severe. Candidate A's show-off and resistance to criticism also concerning.\n\nThus candidate C might be the strongest overall: strong decision quality, stress resilience, technical competence, attention, crew cooperation (concern for others), but communication difficulty and egocentric may be mitigated with training. Also not willing to further education is a concern but maybe less immediate.\n\nWe should present this argument, referencing prior discussion (James and Sarah both advocated A). We can argue that while A has strong technical and leadership, his lack of openness to feedback and unfriendly attitude may be more problematic than C's communication difficulty, especially given C's strong decision-making and crew concern. So we propose shifting vote to Candidate C.\n\nWe need to include explicit facts: C makes correct decisions quickly; C keeps calm in crisis; C has excellent attention skills; C puts concern for others above everything; C difficulty communicating ideas; C regarded as egocentric; C not willing to further education.\n\nWe need to state our vote: Candidate C.\n\nNow produce PUBLIC_MESSAGE and METADATA_JSON with vote."}
